A||||14||Tweek||My fiero refuses to turn over||||1||
Z||000000||Tweek||05-30-2001||04:33 PM||||OK heres the deal...  I have done several diff searches and found some good info and same with the haynes manual, but I am having trouble figuring out which direction to go in.<P>Its an 84 with the stock 2.5 and auto trans. before it stopped turning over altogether, every time I started it, it would turn over slower and slower. Now the started doenst engage, and it doesn't make that clicking sound you sometimes hear. I've tried having someone jump it for like 20-30 mins, but had no result. also having cleaned up the battery connections and a few grounds. because it was a gradual thing Im not sure where to go, im relativly low on the $$ right now..  but I can buy what I nead to to replace whatever is wrong.<P>These are some things I was thinking about, tell me what you think:<BR>The neutral start safty switch<BR>starter soleniod<BR>starter relay<BR>battery (though Im pretty sure its charged good, all the electrics work pretty well including the headlights)<BR>fusible link somewhere maybe melted.<P>When you turn the key the only think that happens is the temp guage pegs to the right, just like it does if you turn the key while in Drive.<P>Woulf it be helpful to get one of those things that you hook up to the battery and starter solonoid to try to start it???   and if this does or does not work what does that prove?<P>I need a little bit of direction, I thought I could figure it out but Im not sure now...   Thanks in advance!<p>[This message has been edited by Tweek (edited 05-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||05-30-2001||04:57 PM||||If you have tilt wheel, tilt it all the way down before you try to start it.<P>Sounds wierd...but just try it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||05-30-2001||05:26 PM||||pull the starter and have it checked before you dig deep into taking other things apart. Maybe if you can get to it just hold cable on starter connector to see if it runs then.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||05-30-2001||06:02 PM||||I was gonna say to just put your cold feet on her back.  Always seems to work for my wife. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||05-30-2001||09:27 PM||||First, try a battery you know to be good,like right off of another car. Don't try to jump your battery,swap batteries.<BR>2nd. It's hard to get at but check the electrical connections at the starter. A loose connection will cause arcing and subsequent corosion. even a good connection can be corroded or "plated" due to the years and location. Check both cables for green corrosion near the connections. I've seen em <BR>corrode in half up in the insulation.<BR>3rd. as suggested, remove the starter and have it checked. Most autozone type places will do it for free. I've seen many starters fail from oil or moisture inside th starter.<BR>good luck<BR>Don <BR>I haven't had to buy or rebuild mine so I don't know what they cost. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||curtis||05-30-2001||09:34 PM||||Tweek, I had the same problem a few weeks ago. I also jumped my battery off for 20-30 minutes. It turned out the battery had a dead cell. Took it to autozone to be checked. Replaced it and now it has no problems starting. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||05-30-2001||10:23 PM||||Battery would be my first, easiest guess, dead cells will keep the battery from charging, and can short the circuit which would prevent the starter from kicking. A little harder, but cheap would be to check and clean all major connections for the battery, alt and starter. I realy doubt the starter is the culperit, even with burnt coils, the solinoid would still kick.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||05-30-2001||11:34 PM||||Hey Tweak!<BR>First try shorting the battery terminal to the start terminal on the solenoid...<BR>If it starts, check the neutral switch and ignition switch...<BR>IF IT DON'T, then starter and solenoid...<BR>When you install a new starter, install a heat shield!<BR>This is very important, as heat is what kills the starter...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000008||SPDFREK||05-31-2001||12:41 AM||||If you have a volt meter, check the battery with just the ignition on, then with someone trying to start it. if it reads fairly close to the same, move on. if it drops badly 1 of 2 things. 1) you have a short. 2) your bat is dead. easy way to test this is take the battery out of another car and swap them.<BR>if it still doesn't work, try having someone crank it while you watch the voltage on the hot wire going to the starter. if it jumps up high, you found the problem. IE=starter.<BR>if you get no voltage at starter, 1 of 3 things. 1) solenoid. 2)neutral safety switch. 3) ignition switch. then there is a 4th, that being fried wires, but you would notice any burt wires when your down there looking at voltage.  I heard once that starters have "locked up" on the flywheel, and hitting the case of the start jars it loose, but you chance screwing things up by hitting them. hope this helps and good luck.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker<P><p>[This message has been edited by SPDFREK (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tweek||05-31-2001||12:10 PM||||Thanks for the replies....  I picked up one of those remote starters and and jumped the terminal on the starter with the other one and then with the + bat terminal. Nothin happened. As the starter connections looked pretty clean (no corrosion that I could see), Im gonna take both the bettery and the starter down to autozone to see what they say about both of em..   thanks!  will report back with progress as I find out.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tweek||05-31-2001||12:11 PM||||Oh..   and Yes, I tried starting it with the steering wheel in all the positions...   =)||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||05-31-2001||12:21 PM||||Damndest thing happend to me the other day while replacing my alt and compressor belts. I was bumping the starter cable when I noticed some arcing going on. Upon further inspection I found the cable had been chafing against the block for some time and has all but been eaten away. I had been having an elusive problem with the car sputteing under hard exceleration, the check engine light would errupt with no codes. It seemed like the engine was just dieing for a split second then come alive again. However if it ate all the way thru the cable I'd have power to the car but no starter. I have not replaced the cable yet but I did put that black wire cover sheething over it till the weekend when I can get a new cable.||||reg||3
Z||000012||84Bill||06-01-2001||12:31 PM||||PUNT!<BR>Whats happening Tweek?<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000013||Tweek||06-01-2001||09:33 PM||||*OUCH*  hehehe<P>Well, I Finally got the atarter out and took it and the bat down to the local discont auto store, had em test both and the starter was toast. I bet it was the oem one too. then I had em test the new one just be sure they were rippin me off and it was a big difference (as in it worked) installed it and Ive never heard it turn over so fast, It starts up much faster than it did before, so all is well...<P><BR>Thanks everyone for your help!<P>-Ted||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroPerformance||06-02-2001||06:37 PM||||If your starter is not working properly check the battery terminals, if those are good then check the grounding wire to your starter.. that is sometimes it.<P>-Charlie<BR>-----<BR>jackalrr@hotmail.com<BR>||||reg||1
